Tongue-Step Footwork

Ingredients: BASSOON + CHICAGO FOOTWORK / JUKE + PREPARED CLAVINET + SNARE / WOODBLOCK + CONTRABASS RECORDER + SHORT MONO SUB

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during an artist residency in a decommissioned municipal turnstile-repair workshop on Chicago’s South Side, where bassoonist Mara Kovács was practicing detached double-tonguing studies while producer DeShawn Bell built 160 BPM footwork patterns on a compact sampler. The breakthrough came when DeShawn muted the obvious snare grid and listened to Mara’s articulations as a rhythm source. A bassoon note was not one event: the tongue released the reed, the air column spoke, the keywork clicked, and the body resonance followed. Instead of sampling those sounds into a drum kit, he kept the bassoon live and placed machine kicks, snares, and chopped percussion in the spaces between its articulation stages. Prepared clavinet, dry woodblock, contrabass recorder, and a short mono sub completed a style where reed speech becomes the skeleton of a high-speed dance groove.

Tongue-Step Footwork is a dry, high-velocity acoustic-electronic form built from the mechanics of bassoon articulation against the fractured rhythmic logic of Chicago footwork. Footwork supplies the chassis: 154–166 BPM, rapid kick displacement, clipped snares, syncopated tom and rim figures, short vocal fragments, sudden half-beat holes, and sub-bass patterns that imply several simultaneous dance accents without resolving into four-on-the-floor. The bassoon supplies a contrasting physical logic through reed attack, tongue release, breath pressure, key-click latency, overblown register shifts, and the unequal response time of notes across the instrument.

The bassoon is recorded with one small-diaphragm condenser near the bell and keywork, one ribbon microphone aimed across the tenor joint for body and low-mid warmth, and one close side microphone positioned to capture tongue noise and breath without overwhelming the pitched sound. The condenser captures transient edge, pad clicks, finger timing, and register changes. The ribbon gives the instrument physical wood and bore resonance. The side microphone is automated upward only during articulation-heavy sections.

Players favor staccato root-fifth cells, minor-second turns, repeated-note bursts, low-register pedal notes, octave pops, split-register leaps, short multiphonic edges, slap-like tongue attacks, breath-only releases, and rapid double-tongued patterns. Long lyrical legato is reduced. The defining gesture is a note that can be heard as several sequential physical events rather than one smooth tone.

The central compositional device is the Tongue Chain. Each important bassoon articulation is treated as RELEASE, SPEAK, CLICK, and BODY. RELEASE is the tongue leaving the reed. SPEAK is the pitched onset. CLICK is the associated key or pad transient when present. BODY is the short resonance that follows inside the bore and wooden body. Electronic drum events are placed around these phases by ear; no transient detector or MIDI conversion determines the groove.

During RELEASE, upper percussion is often absent so the physical tongue attack remains audible. SPEAK can coincide with kick or sub for direct weight. CLICK is the preferred position for clipped snare, rim, or woodblock. BODY is often left clear or answered by a short contrabass-recorder note. By rotating which phase receives the electronic accent, one repeated bassoon figure can create several different footwork grids.

A second device, False Double, alternates between genuine bassoon double-tonguing and programmed drum doubles that imitate the perceived spacing without copying the acoustic sound. The listener repeatedly mistakes machine repetition for instrumental articulation and vice versa. The two sources remain timbrally distinct; the illusion comes from timing, not sample replacement.

A third device, Register Trap, exploits the fact that identical fingering density can produce different response speed across bassoon registers. Low notes speak more slowly and heavily; upper notes can appear sharper and earlier. The drum grid stays unchanged while the player moves the same rhythmic figure through low, middle, and upper registers, causing perceived groove placement to shift without any quantization change.

Footwork drums remain sharp and economical. Kicks are compact, frequently syncopated in clusters rather than continuous. Snares are short and dry. Rims, toms, and woodblock hits provide secondary grids. Hats appear in broken sixteenth patterns with deliberate omissions. Vocal chops are optional and should be extremely short, functioning as punctuation rather than lyrical hooks. Drum density can be high, but the mix must preserve enough empty upper-mid space for bassoon articulation.

The short mono sub uses root, fifth, octave, and minor-seventh pulses with fast release. It does not mirror every kick. Sub is most effective when it reinforces SPEAK on low bassoon notes or arrives one subdivision after CLICK to create a displaced body-weight sensation. Long 808 glides are excluded.

Prepared clavinet supplies dry harmonic teeth. Selected strings are muted with removable felt, paper, or hand damping. Parts use dead-note sixteenths, narrow dyads, suspended seconds, open fifths, octave taps, and repeated one-note ticks. Clavinet is frequently placed after CLICK but before BODY, occupying the smallest available midrange gap without creating a continuous chord bed.

Snare drum and woodblock provide acoustic punctuation. The snare is tuned high and damped heavily, used for isolated rimshots, dead center taps, brushes against the head, and short cross-stick figures. Woodblock provides a harder transient that can masquerade as keywork at high speed. These instruments replace selected machine hits rather than doubling them continuously.

Contrabass recorder supplies the low acoustic counterfield. It uses breath-heavy fundamentals, finger-hole noise, short overblown fifths, low octave pulses, and air-only attacks. It is not a bassoon substitute. Its wider, softer onset contrasts with the bassoon’s reed-and-key precision and becomes especially important in sections where sub-bass disappears.

Vocals are optional and minimal. English, Hungarian, or Polish fragments may reference turnstile numbers, fare tokens, latch faults, repair logs, inspection stamps, or commands such as “release,” “click,” “open,” and “wrong gate.” Chopped phrases should remain intelligible enough to feel documentary rather than purely percussive.

Effects remain tightly controlled. Bassoon stays mostly dry with a short tiled-room reflection and occasional single-repeat delay on phrase endings. Clavinet gets a tiny spring or plate. Contrabass recorder receives a dark room. Drums use firm bus compression with fast release. Sub remains mono. Long reverb tails, trance risers, granular reed clouds, orchestral bassoon layering, and cinematic woodwind pads are excluded.

The defining tension is speech mechanics versus dance mechanics. The reed articulates like a mouth; the drum machine answers like a foot.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 154–166 BPM, primarily 4/4 Chicago footwork / juke architecture with displaced kicks, clipped snares, broken sixteenth hats, tom/rim secondary grids, and bassoon figures that cross the pulse through rapid double-tonguing and register-dependent response.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian, Aeolian, Phrygian-adjacent color, open fifths, minor sevenths, suspended seconds, narrow semitone tension, octave pedals, and long static tonal centers that allow articulation and register to carry form.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Tongue Chain — Bassoon articulations are heard as RELEASE → SPEAK → CLICK → BODY and the electronic rhythm is composed around those phases.
  2. False Double — Live double-tonguing and programmed drum doubles exchange rhythmic identity without timbral imitation.
  3. Register Trap — The same rhythmic figure shifts perceived timing when moved through different bassoon registers while the drum grid stays fixed.
  4. Keywork Clearance — Upper-mid percussion thins around exposed bassoon key and pad transients.
  5. Short-Sub Rule — Mono sub supports selected articulation points but never forms a continuous 808 tail.
  6. No-Reed-Sampling Rule — The principal bassoon part remains live; reed attacks cannot be reduced to a generic chopped sample kit.
- Dynamics: Intensity grows through faster tongue alternation, wider register jumps, denser kick displacement, sharper woodblock/snare substitution, more active clavinet dead notes, and shorter gaps between bassoon and machine responses. Climaxes remain dry, agile, and rhythmically legible rather than huge.

Scene & Origin Lore
Tongue-Step Footwork formed inside a retired municipal turnstile-repair and fare-equipment workshop on Chicago’s South Side. The building retained waist-high turnstile frames, mechanical latches, token trays, stamped inspection cards, steel parts bins, green maintenance benches, cable reels, numbered gate mechanisms, fluorescent lights, and a tiled test corridor where repaired units were once cycled thousands of times.

Bassoonist Mara Kovács had been using the corridor to practice articulation because the hard walls made every tongue release and key click obvious. She noticed that fast detached passages changed character depending on register even when the written rhythm remained identical.

Producer DeShawn Bell was working nearby on skeletal footwork tracks. His first attempt sampled Mara’s bassoon into one-shot stabs and sounded generic. He deleted the samples, recorded her live, and removed several machine hits so the tongue and keywork could occupy their places. One repeated figure suddenly seemed to contain an extra percussionist inside the instrument.

Prepared-clavinet player Nia Brooks joined after designing dead-note parts that could sit between key clicks without obscuring them. Percussionist Tomasz Zieliński reduced his setup to one damped snare and two woodblocks so acoustic transients remained distinguishable from the sampler. Contrabass-recorder player Elena Varga completed the group with low air-weight for sub-free passages.

Their first performances were called gate tests. A projected diagram behind the band showed four blocks—RELEASE, SPEAK, CLICK, BODY—while a separate row showed the footwork kick and snare grid. The diagram was not synchronized to performance; it was a listening map. Audience members learned to hear the bassoon as a chain of mechanical events instead of a continuous orchestral voice.

The visual language developed around turnstile chrome, maintenance green, fare-token brass, fluorescent white, rubber black, inspection-card pink, bassoon maple, and dull red warning labels. The unofficial rule is that if the bassoon could be replaced by a synth lead without collapsing the groove, the arrangement is too generic.

