Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Gate Six Opens After the Key Click
Story & Context: At 162 BPM, Mara begins with a repeated low-mid bassoon figure using short double-tongued tonic and fifth attacks. DeShawn leaves the first CLICK position empty for four cycles, then places a clipped snare exactly where the key transient lands. Nia adds one dead clavinet sus2 after every second CLICK. Tomasz replaces one machine rim with a real woodblock hit so closely matched in timing that the source becomes difficult to identify. Elena remains silent until the mono sub drops out, then adds one low contrabass-recorder fundamental under BODY. In the middle section Mara moves the same rhythm one octave higher; the notes speak faster and the unchanged drum grid suddenly feels late, creating the first full Register Trap.
Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Stainless turnstile gate marked 6 half-open under fluorescent light, a bassoon key mechanism enlarged like an engineering diagram, four small timing labels—RELEASE, SPEAK, CLICK, BODY—running beneath the gate arm. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “gate six,” “hear the click,” “open after,” and “wrong timing.”

Track 2: Token Tray Repeats the False Double
Story & Context: At 166 BPM, DeShawn programs the EP’s densest kick pattern but leaves unusually large upper-mid gaps. Mara alternates genuine double-tongued repeated notes with single articulated notes while DeShawn inserts programmed snare doubles after selected singles. The listener repeatedly hears two attacks without immediately knowing whether the second came from the bassoon or the sampler. Nia uses prepared-clavinet dead notes only between pairs. Tomasz contributes dry rim clicks at section boundaries. Elena plays air-only contrabass-recorder attacks during a central sub-free passage. The arrangement peaks by layering three False Double exchanges in succession, then drops to one completely dry bassoon note.
Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Brass fare-token tray containing paired tokens except for one single token in the center, a waveform showing two near-identical rhythmic attacks from different sources, silver bassoon key cups reflected in brushed metal. Spoken fragments limited to “two for one,” “false double,” “same gap,” and “which one moved.”

Track 3: Maintenance Corridor Moves the Downbeat Upstairs
Story & Context: The closer runs at 156 BPM and starts with Mara in the lowest practical register, where each note has a heavy, slightly delayed bloom. DeShawn uses a restrained kick/snare grid and almost no hats. Every sixteen bars Mara moves the same rhythmic figure upward through the instrument while the machine pattern remains fixed. Register Trap progressively shifts the perceived groove forward until the upper-register version seems to pull the beat ahead of itself. Nia marks each register change with one dry clavinet octave. Tomasz replaces a single snare with an acoustic cross-stick after each shift. Elena sustains soft recorder fundamentals only during the lowest register. In the final minute Mara descends back to the opening register one level at a time; the groove seems to slow without any tempo change. The track ends on one audible key closure with no drum response.
Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Long tiled maintenance corridor with numbered turnstile mechanisms climbing an impossible staircase, one bassoon fingering diagram shifting upward beside a fixed drum grid, fluorescent perspective lines converging at a dark gate. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “same rhythm,” “move upstairs,” “grid stays,” and “close the key.”

